          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %strong cartesian bicategory
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory"} bicategory
            with properties that make it behave like a bicategory of generalized
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relations"} relations
          %p Examples of cartesian bicategories include
          %ul
            %li
              the bicategory
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rel"} Rel
              of sets and relations,
            %li
              more generally, the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory+of+relations"} bicategory of relations
              of a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+category"} regular category
            %li
              the bicategory
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Span"} Span
              of sets and spans,
            %li
              more generally, the bicategory of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spans"} spans
              in a finitely
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+category"} complete category
            %li
              the bicategory
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profunctor"} Prof
              of (small) categories and profunctors,
            %li
              more generally, the bicategory of internal categories and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profunctors"}> profunctors
              ) in a finitely complete category
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %li
              as an alternative generalization, the bicategory of enriched categories and modules over a
              %em cartesian
              monoidal category
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          %p Non-examples include
          %ul
            %li
              the locally-discrete bicategory
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
              of sets and functions,
            %li
              the bicategory
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
              of categories and functors,
            %li
              the bicategory of profunctors and enriched in a
              %em noncartesian
              monoidal category
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          %p
            Consider for example the bicategory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rel"} Rel
            %em not
            the categorical product, this symmetric monoidal structure is
            %em not
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian monoidal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            of sets and functions, on which it
            %em is
            the categorical product. Note that a relation is the graph of a function if and only if it possesses a right adjoint (which is given by the opposite relation). In a general bicategory, a 1-cell is called
            %em map-like
            if it possesses a right adjoint, and these are the 1-cells playing the role that functions play in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Rel
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Rel
          %p
            Generalizing this situation, a cartesian bicategory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            is a symmetric monoidal bicategory for which the monoidal product restricts to the categorical product on the sub-bicategory of map-like 1-cells, subject to natural compatibility conditions. Remarkably, the structure of such a tensor is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/property-like+structure"} property-like
            (is essentially unique when it exists). Thus the definition can be formulated directly in terms of the bicategory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            without mentioning a symmetric monoidal structure at all. Not only is this technically convenient (as the precise definition of a symmetric monoidal bicategory is quite technical), but it is moreover a very natural perspective to take.
          %p
            Like allegories, cartesian bicategories provide a convenient abstract setting in which to study the calculus of relations, but unlike allegories they embrace higher examples like
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Span
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Span
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Mod
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mod
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Todd+Trimble"} Todd Trimble
          %h2#technical_preliminaries Technical preliminaries
          %p Note: All compositions will be written in the traditional order, in which application proceeds from right to left.
          %p We work with familiar notions of the theory of bicategories (which, for reasons of consonance with 2-terminology, we also call 2-categories) but in some cases under new names. We calculate with pasting diagrams in 2-categories as if they were strict 2-categories.
          %p
            Our notion of morphism between 2-categories has gone under various names: “homomorphism” in the sense of Bénabou, also known as “pseudofunctor” or weak 2-functor, where the structural constraints are isomorphisms. Here they are simply called
            = succeed "." do
              %strong 2-functors
          %p
            Each 2-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            gives rise to a hom 2-functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi hom
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi B
                      %mi op
                    %mo ×
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} hom: B^{op} \times B \to Cat
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(-, -)
          %p
            Given 2-functors
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo ,
                    %mi G
                    %mo :
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F, G: B \to C
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+natural+transformation"} lax notion of morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi θ
                  %mo :
                  %mi F
                  %mo →
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta: F \to G
            between 2-functors for which the structural cells
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi θ
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta \cdot f
            (for 1-cells
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi a
                  %mo →
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: a \to b
            in
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi F
                          %mi a
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mi θ
                              %mi a
                        %mtd
                          %mi G
                          %mi a
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi F
                          %mi f
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ⇒
                            %mrow
                              %mi θ
                              %mo ⋅
                              %mi f
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mi G
                          %mi f
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi F
                          %mi b
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mi θ
                              %mi b
                        %mtd
                          %mi G
                          %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  F a &amp; \overset{\theta a}{\to} &amp; G a\\
                  F f \downarrow &amp; \overset{\theta \cdot f}{\Rightarrow} &amp; \downarrow G f\\
                  F b &amp; \underset{\theta b}{\to} &amp; G b
                  }
          %p
            These are called “oplax transformations” by some authors such as Bénabou and “lax transformations” by other authors such as Johnstone; on this page we will simply call them (2-)
            %strong> transformations
            \. A transformation is
            %strong strong
            if the structural cells
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi θ
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta \cdot f
            are isomorphisms.
          %p
            There is a well-known notion of morphism between transformation which has been called
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modification"} modification
            = succeed "." do
              %strong 2-transfors
          %p
            In any case, given 2-categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
