          %h3#essential_uniqueness_of_cartesian_structure Essential uniqueness of cartesian structure
          %p
            So far we have seen that for a cartesian bicategory
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Map
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Map(B)
                carries 2-products,
            %li
              %p Local hom-categories carry products.
          %p
            It is clear that 2-product data on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Map
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Map(B)
            and local product data on local hom-categories are uniquely determined up to appropriate notions of equivalence, since they are determined by appropriate universal properties. Now we show that these data actually determine the whole of the cartesian structure. We may therefore conclude that a cartesian structure on a bicategory must be essentially unique, if it exists.
          %p
            To explain how this works, it helps to consider some simple examples of cartesian bicategories such as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Rel
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Rel
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo :
                  %mi B
                  %mo ×
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes: B \times B \to B
          %p
            from the data above. Suppose given two 1-cells
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                    %mo :
                    %mi a
                    %mo →
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r: a \to b
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                  %mo :
                  %mi c
                  %mo →
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s: c \to d
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r \subseteq b
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s \subseteq d
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r \otimes s
            is intuitively a “rectangle”, like
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                  %mo ×
                  %mi s
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi b
                  %mo ×
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r \times s \subseteq b \times d
            in the case of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Rel
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Rel
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
            along the projection
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo :
                    %mi b
                    %mo ×
                    %mi d
                    %mo →
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1: b \times d \to b
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
            along the projection
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 2
                    %mo :
                    %mi b
                    %mo ×
                    %mi d
                    %mo →
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_2: b \times d \to d
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi s
                  %mo ≅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mi π
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo ,
                      %mi d
                    %mo *
                  %mi r
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo ×
                    %mi loc
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mi π
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo ,
                      %mi d
                    %mo *
                  %mi s
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mo ,
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r \otimes s \cong (\pi_{1, b, d}^{\ast} r \pi_{1, a, c}) \times_{loc} (\pi_{2, b, d}^{\ast} s \pi_{2, a, c})
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
            play the role of mere placeholders.
          %p
            It is a theorem (whose proof we defer) is that in a cartesian bicategory, we may reconstruct the functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} - \otimes -
            by the formula
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo ×
                    %mi loc
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mi π
                    %mn 2
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\pi_{1}^{\ast} (-) \pi_{1}) \times_{loc} (\pi_{2}^{\ast} (-) \pi_{2})
          %p
            where each of the displayed data is manifestly part of the 2-product structure on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Map
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Map(B)
            or the local cartesian structure.
          %p
            The other data we are required to reconstruct are structure cells like
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                    %mi r
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta r
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi r
                  %mo ,
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi \langle r, s\rangle
            which pertain to the transformations
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                    %mi r
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta r
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                  %mi r
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi δ
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi r
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi r
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi r
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi δ
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta r: (\delta b)r \to (r \otimes r)(\delta a)
          %p which is mated to a 2-cell
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
